                    {
                        "sense_id": "5f00ab94-c3f8-39ec-ba7d-a785c49e2dd6",
                        "entry_id": "784fae0a-d4eb-31e1-a7e5-85b1be31bb33",
                        "glosses": [
                            "The tip of the coronoid process of the mandible."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "3aab796d-e2e8-4cff-8680-ed16505ad848",
                                "sense_id": "5f00ab94-c3f8-39ec-ba7d-a785c49e2dd6",
                                "text": "It has been demonstrated that practically every patient under a general anaesthetic has traces of blood below the larynx, while in almost 50 per cent of the cases this strain extends to the coronion."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "0c0f6d63-6143-4d54-80ca-6a09fe350618",
                                "sense_id": "5f00ab94-c3f8-39ec-ba7d-a785c49e2dd6",
                                "text": "Common to both subtypes are, to mention this briefly, a low, padded forehead ; rather pronounced coronions ; and a small mouth with curved, fairly fleshy lips."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "ff4c8dd0-8f6f-4e64-94e7-6b5fd7b89e52",
                                "sense_id": "5f00ab94-c3f8-39ec-ba7d-a785c49e2dd6",
                                "text": "The jaw of Callithrix, by contrast, shows a less aggressive coronion and a condylar pivot which lies nearer to the occlusal plane, reflecting the marmosets' greater emphasis on exudate-gouging, although not to Cebuella's extreme."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
